  "enhanced_information": "Cholesterol is synthesized in the liver and needs to be transported to peripheral tissues. The primary carrier of cholesterol in the bloodstream is Low-Density Lipoprotein (LDL), which delivers cholesterol to various tissues. While Very Low-Density Lipoprotein (VLDL) primarily carries triglycerides, it is converted to LDL after triglyceride removal, further supporting LDL's role in cholesterol transport. Chylomicrons are involved in transporting dietary lipids from the intestines, not cholesterol from the liver. Therefore, the correct answer is LDL, as it is the main transporter of cholesterol to peripheral tissues.",
